From 25fa34ba15bec5c56389f4ceaddfd3d749f0b608 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Levine <blahm1d@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 13:33:07 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] core: P0013 - don't abort an in-flight fetch when an
 interrupt arrives

CORE_FETCH diverts to the interrupt-entry sequence combinationally whenever
int_take is high. If int_take rises AFTER the fetch request was already
latched by the memory system (e.g. the display interrupt firing mid-fetch,
now possible since P0012 made INTPEND.DI async to the CPU), the core
abandons the fetch and jumps to CORE_INT_PUSH_PC. The memsys's ack for the
aborted read then lands while the core drives the return-PC write, so the
core mistakes the read-ack for its write-ack -> the PC push is never
committed. The return-PC stack slot keeps its old value (0), the ST push
commits fine, and RETI later returns to 0 -> derail into VRAM after ~18
frames.

Fix: track fetch_inflight_q (CORE_FETCH holds mem_req, no ack yet) and gate
the interrupt divert on !fetch_inflight_q. An in-flight fetch now completes;
the interrupt is taken at the next fetch boundary (HW-correct). Post-fix the
boot runs the interrupt-driven main loop with no derail through 6M cycles;
frame-advances track render-calls 1:1 (was 6084 vs 18). MM unit TBs pass.

Also clarifies the INT_VEC_* comments (values unchanged; verified against
MAME check_interrupt: DI=0xFFFFFEA0, HI=0xFFFFFEC0, WV=0xFFFFFE80).

