+## P0013 — an interrupt taken mid-fetch aborts the fetch → dropped PC push → RETI derail
+
+**Status:** ✅ APPLIED + VERIFIED 2026-07-05. `grep -rn P0013 rtl/`.
+
+**Applied edits (`rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv`):**
+- New `fetch_inflight_q` register: set while `CORE_FETCH` holds `mem_req` with no ack,
+  cleared on `mem_ack` or on leaving `CORE_FETCH`.
+- `CORE_FETCH` interrupt gate: `if (int_take)` → `if (int_take && !fetch_inflight_q)`.
+- (`rtl/tms34010_pkg.sv`: only the INT_VEC_* comments were clarified — the vector
+  VALUES were already correct; see the false start below.)
+
+**Problem.** `CORE_FETCH` asserts `mem_req` for the instruction fetch and, combinationally
+each cycle, diverts to the interrupt-entry sequence if `int_take` is high. If `int_take`
+rises AFTER the fetch request was already latched by the memory system (e.g. the display
+interrupt firing mid-fetch — newly possible once **P0012** made `INTPEND.DI` asynchronous
+to the CPU), the core abandons the fetch and jumps to `CORE_INT_PUSH_PC`. But the memsys
+is still finishing the aborted read; its `mem_ack` then lands while the core is in
+`CORE_INT_PUSH_PC` driving the return-PC WRITE, so the core mistakes the read-ack for its
+write-ack and advances to `CORE_INT_PUSH_ST` — **the PC push is never latched/committed by
+the memsys.** The return-PC stack slot keeps its old value (0), the ST push (next word)
+commits fine, and `RETI` later returns to `0x00000000` → derail into VRAM.
+
+**Evidence (cycle trace, `sim/tb_yunit_intstate.sv`).** At the derailing interrupt:
+`cyc N` CORE_FETCH issues `mem_req` (read `ffe02090`); `cyc N+1` DI pends → `int_take`↑ →
+abort to PUSH_PC; `cyc N+2` PUSH_PC drives `@010fff90 <= ffe02090` and sees `ack=1` in the
+SAME cycle (that ack is the aborted read's), but `ram[FFF9]` stays `0000`; the following
+PUSH_ST (`@010fff70 <= ST`) commits normally (RETI reads ST back correctly). A different
+interrupt with the identical SP0 but no mid-fetch collision pushed + returned fine — same
+address, different outcome, i.e. a handshake race, not addressing. Post-fix: the in-flight
+fetch completes, the instruction executes, the interrupt is taken at the next boundary
+(HW-correct), the PC push commits, and the boot runs the interrupt-driven main loop with
+**NO derail through 6M cycles** — frame-advances (114) now track render-calls (114) 1:1
+(was 6084 vs 18), ~one display interrupt per frame.
+
+**False start (recorded so nobody repeats it):** the derail first *looked* like a wrong DI
+vector — the DI vectors to `0xFFFFFEA0`, which I assumed "should" be `0xFFFFFF00" per a
+mis-remembered vector map. Changing it hid the derail (routed DI to a benign short handler)
+but was WRONG. **MAME `tms34010.cpp check_interrupt()` is authoritative: HI=0xFFFFFEC0,
+DI=0xFFFFFEA0, WV=0xFFFFFE80, INT1=0xFFFFFFC0, INT2=0xFFFFFFA0, NMI=0xFFFFFEE0** — exactly
+the core's original constants. Reverted; the real bug was the fetch-abort handshake above.
+
+**Upstream note.** Real core bug, not Y-unit-specific — any interrupt that becomes pending
+while a fetch is outstanding hits it. birdybro's per-instruction TBs never drove an async
+interrupt during a fetch, so it stayed hidden until a real game (P0012) did. Offer upstream.
+
+---
+
 ## P0012 — wire the display-interrupt source (INTEGRATION, was upstream Task 0097)
 
 **Status:** ✅ APPLIED 2026-07-05 (integration, not a bug fix). `grep -rn P0012 rtl/`.
diff --git a/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv b/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
index 5f4abe2..b8b700d 100644
--- a/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
+++ b/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
@@ -2293,6 +2293,25 @@ module tms34010_core
   logic int_take;
   assign int_take = nmi_req || int_req;
 
+  // P0013: a fetch request the memory system has already latched MUST run to
+  // completion before we honour an interrupt. Otherwise, if int_take rises after
+  // mem_req was asserted (e.g. the display interrupt firing mid-fetch — possible
+  // once P0012 made INTPEND.DI async to the CPU), the core would abort the fetch
+  // and jump to CORE_INT_PUSH_PC; the memsys's ack for the aborted fetch then
+  // gets misattributed to the interrupt's PC push, so that 32-bit write is
+  // silently dropped and RETI returns to a corrupt (0) address → derail. Once a
+  // fetch is in flight, ignore int_take until mem_ack; the interrupt is taken at
+  // the next fetch boundary — HW-correct (the in-flight instruction completes).
+  logic fetch_inflight_q;
+  always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
+    if (rst)
+      fetch_inflight_q <= 1'b0;
+    else if (state_q == CORE_FETCH && mem_req && !mem_ack)
+      fetch_inflight_q <= 1'b1;
+    else if (mem_ack || state_q != CORE_FETCH)
+      fetch_inflight_q <= 1'b0;
+  end
+
   // Latched state for the entry sequence (captured when leaving CORE_FETCH):
   //   int_vec_q    — the trap-vector address to fetch.
   //   int_is_nmi_q — this entry is an NMI (drives the auto-clear).
@@ -2526,7 +2545,9 @@ module tms34010_core
         // enabled INTPEND bit). When taken, do NOT fetch — pc_value stays at the
         // resume address. An NMI with NMIM=1 saves no context and jumps
         // straight to the vector; everything else pushes PC+ST first.
-        if (int_take) begin
+        // P0013: gate on !fetch_inflight_q so an interrupt that arrives after the
+        // fetch request was already latched doesn't abort it (see fetch_inflight_q).
+        if (int_take && !fetch_inflight_q) begin
           state_d = (nmi_req && nmi_nmim) ? CORE_INT_VECTOR : CORE_INT_PUSH_PC;
         end else begin
           mem_req  = 1'b1;
